  "verbalization": "The visible evidence from the hetionet dataset strongly suggests a mechanistic link between Glycine and Glrb, primarily through the involvement of Glra subunits in ion channel activity. Glycine binds to Glra3 and Glra2, which are part of extracellular-glycine-gated or extracellular-glycine-gated chloride channels. These channels are then participated by Glrb, indicating a direct interaction between Glycine and Glrb via these subunits. The high scores in all three paths (0.7763) suggest a consistent and robust relationship, with Glycine acting as a ligand that modulates the activity of Glra subunits, which in turn influence Glrb's function. The shared pathway involving extracellular-glycine-gated ion channels implies that Glycine may regulate Glrb through a common mechanism, possibly by altering the activity of ion channels that Glrb is part of. This relationship is supported by the presence of multiple binding and participation events, reinforcing the notion that Glycine exerts its effects on Glrb through a well-defined, biologically plausible mechanism.",
